WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.976
A detector’s probability of a positive result given a real target is different from the probability

00:00:05.976 --> 00:00:11.753
of a real target given a positive result. The two condition on different populations, so one

00:00:11.753 --> 00:00:14.280
cannot simply be renamed as the other.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.872
A is the target class; B is a positive prediction. The false-positive probability is conditional on

00:00:20.872 --> 00:00:22.390
not having the target.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:29.635
Multiply each conditional probability by the size of its source population. Both true and false positives

00:00:29.635 --> 00:00:32.160
contribute to the observed positive group.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:38.420
Will a rarer target increase or decrease the probability that a positive result is correct? Pause

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.937
here and make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:52.034
The denominator includes the two mutually exclusive routes. It is not just the true-positive rate. For

00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:58.887
our starting inputs, True-positive joint: 0.16. False-positive joint: 0.08. Posterior: 0.66667.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:05.890
Among all positive predictions, this fraction really belongs to the target class. Prevalence can change that

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.730
fraction even when test behaviour stays fixed.

00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:13.223
Ask “among which group?” every time you read a conditional probability.

